\subsection{Out-of-domain experiments}

In out-of-domain experiments, we evaluate ViSymRe on several databases released by SRBench \cite{la2021contemporary}, including $119$ Feynman \cite{udrescu2020ai} problems, $14$  ODE-Strogatz \cite{strogatz2018nonlinear} problems and $122$ black box regression problems with unknown underlying equations. In addition, a new challenging benchmark SRSD-Feynman has been published recently, which resamples the 119 Feynman problems in a new range \cite{matsubara2022rethinking}. The details of the used databases are described in \ref{Details of the used databases}.

The sample points for each problem are randomly split into 75\% training and 25\% testing. Specially, for SRSD-Feynman problems, we use the training and test set divided by the publisher. For black box regression problems, we only use the instances with continuous features and less than $10$ dimensions, because the pre-trained ViSymRe and E2E support a maximum of $10$ input variables. 

ViSymRe is trained on a training set containing randomly generated $5$ million equation skeletons with up to 10 variables. The Adam optimizer is used to minimize the loss, and the learning rate is adjusted using cosine annealing strategy. For the other parameters of ViSymRe used in testing, including the number of bags, the number of test points per bag, beam size and BFGS-related parameters, see Table \ref{table:default parameters employed in test}. We compare ViSymRe with state-of-the-art symbolic regression models, including \cite{schmidt2010age, mcconaghy2011ffx, arnaldo2014multiple, la2016epsilon, virgolin2017scalable, 2018Learning, jin2019bayesian, petersen2019deep, virgolin2019linear, udrescu2020ai,  burlacu2020operon, de2021interaction, kamienny2022end,  landajuela2022unified, cranmer2023interpretable, shojaee2024transformer}, the results of which are referenced from \cite{la2021contemporary, matsubara2022rethinking, kamienny2022end, shojaee2024transformer}.

For the benchmark of 119 Feynman problems, we evaluate symbolic regression models in terms of $R^2$ accuracy and equation complexity. Fig. \ref{fem} presents the results. We can see that ViSymRe achieves the best overall performance. Unlike purely numeric-driven models that are easy to overfit or generate overly complex equations, the equations predicted by ViSymRe are not only accurate but also simple. Since the Feynman problems approximate the distribution of natural laws observed in the real-world, strong performance on this benchmark indicates that ViSymRe has the potential to reveal interpretable equations from observational or experimental data. 


