\subsection{Support points sampling}

For each equation skeleton, we first sample constants following the distribution in Table \ref{tab:equation_generator}, and then sample $n$ points for each variable. To enhance the diversity of sample distributions, we divide $n$ points into $k$ clusters ($k \sim \mathcal{U}\{1, k_{{max}}\}$ with $k_{max}$ is generally 5) \cite{kamienny2022end}. Points within each cluster are sampled from a Uniform  distribution whose maximum and minimum values are randomly determined. The minimum values follow an exponential distribution (with $\lambda=1/2$), and the difference between the maximum and minimum values is distributed according to a normal distribution ($\mathcal{N}(5, 5)$). Fig \ref{histogram} shows the histograms of the minimum and maximum values. For each cluster, 30\% of the points are randomly selected to be negative. After the points of all variables are sampled, they are substituted into the equation to calculate the target. A complete set of support points is formatted as $D = \{(x_{1_i},\cdots,x_{d_i}, y_i)\}_{i=1}^n \in R^{n\times (d+1)}$. When the model is designed to support more than $d$ variables, the points of remaining variables are padded with 0s.

