\section{Details of the used databases}
\label{Details of the used databases}
\textbf{Feynman database:} The Feynman database comes from first principles models of physical systems, representing the static systems. It contains 119 symbolic regression problems from the Feynman Lectures on Physics \cite{feynman2015feynman}, which are recently compiled into datasets that serve as a benchmark for evaluating symbolic regression models \cite{udrescu2020ai}.

\textbf{ODE-Strogatz database:} The ODE-Strogatz database \cite{strogatz2018nonlinear} also comes from first principles models of physical systems, representing the non-linear and chaotic dynamical processes. It comprises 14 problems, each detailing the trajectory of a 2-state system governed by a first-order ordinary differential equation (ODE). 

\textbf{Black box regression database:} The black box regression problems originate from various open-source repositories, such as OpenML \cite{vanschoren2014openml} and the UCI repository \cite{asuncion2007uci}. They comprise 46 ``real-world" problems (collected from true physical processes) and 76 synthetic problems (generated from static equations or simulations).

\textbf{SRSD-Feynman database:} SRSD-Feynman \cite{matsubara2022rethinking} is an improved benchmark of Feynman database (FSRD) \cite{udrescu2020ai}. Here are the key distinctions of them:
\begin{itemize}
	\item In SRSD, physical constants such as the speed of light and gravitational are treated as true constants, whereas in FSRD, they are treated as variables. 
	\item Some duplicate problems in FSRD have identical equation and sampling range, which are addressed in SRSD by treating them as separate problems. 
	\item SRSD defines sampling ranges based on typical physics experiments, using SI units that are more appropriate for confirming physical phenomena. 
\end{itemize}


\section{Additional pseudo-codes}
\label{Additional algorithms}

Algorithm \ref{Search ancestor nodes} is used to monitor the ancestor nodes of the current position in the decoding process, to limit the structure of the outputted equations. This algorithm has the potential to capture more information from the ancestor nodes, such as units and frequencies, to further promote dimensional consistency of the output equations, which we will consider in future work.

\begin{algorithm}[ht]
	\caption{Ancestor nodes search algorithm}
	\label{Search ancestor nodes}
	\begin{algorithmic}[1]
		\State \textbf{Input:} $\mathcal{T}:$ Current equation  tree
		\State \textbf{Output:} ${\mathcal{N}:}$  Ancestor nodes
		\Function{search\_ancestor\_nodes}{$\mathcal{T}$}
		\State ${\mathcal{N}} \gets []$\Comment{Store ancestor nodes}
		\State child\_counter $ \gets []$\Comment{Track the number of child nodes}
		\For{ node $\in$  $\mathcal{T}$} \Comment{Traverse the nodes in the tree}
		\If{node $\in$ Unary Operators}
		\State $\mathcal{N} \gets$ $\mathcal{N} \cup$ node
		\State child\_counter $\gets$ child\_counter $\cup$ 1 \Comment{Add 1 to child\_counter for unary operations}
		\ElsIf{node $\in$ Binary Operators}
		\State $\mathcal{N} \gets$ $\mathcal{N} \cup$ node
		\State child\_counter $\gets$ child\_counter $\cup$ 2 \Comment{Add 2 to child\_counter for binary operations}
		\ElsIf{node  $\in$ Variable \textbf{or} node  $\in$ Constant}
		\If {child\_counter $\neq$ $\emptyset$}
		\State child\_counter[-1] $\gets$ child\_counter[-1] - 1   \Comment{Subtract 1 to child\_counter for variable and constant}

		\EndIf
		\EndIf
			\While{ child\_counter $\neq$ $\emptyset$ \textbf{and} child\_counter[-1] = 0}
		\State {$\mathcal{N}$ $\gets$ $\mathcal{N} \setminus \mathcal{N}[-1]$}
		\State {child\_counter $\gets$ child\_counter $\setminus$ child\_counter[-1]}
		\State child\_counter[-1] $\gets$ child\_counter[-1] - 1
		\EndWhile
		\EndFor
		\State \Return ${\mathcal{N}}$   \Comment {Return the ancestor nodes of the current position}
		\EndFunction
		\State $\mathcal{N} \gets$  \Call{search\_ancestor\_nodes}{$\mathcal{T}$}

	\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
