As Intelligent Transportation Systems (ITS) evolved beyond onboard sensing, the development of communication technologies became a key enabler. This led to the integration of Roadside Units (RSUs) into transportation systems \cite{kouonchie2022roadside}. Before modern RSU systems were developed, infrastructure units primarily relied on Dedicated Short-Range Communications (DSRC) using the IEEE 802.11p standard. This approach helped mitigate perception occlusion caused by large trucks and allowed traffic participants to share roadside sensors - offering a more cost-effective solution compared to equipping each vehicle individually \cite{newamerica_dsrc_failure}.

However, this setup still faced several shortcomings. DSRC was designed for limited communication ranges, requiring RSUs to be deployed closely together, which reduced cost efficiency. Maintenance was also difficult due to the lack of standardized protocols and the reliance on custom-made equipment. Furthermore, the computational power of these systems was limited, and they provided minimal support for cybersecurity. These limitations underscored the need for more scalable, long-range, and standardized communication infrastructures to meet the growing demands of ITS.

With the emergence of satellite communication technologies, ITS has begun to incorporate satellite connectivity into its operation \cite{shu2023group}. By enabling information exchange among RSUs, control centers, ground stations, and potentially connected vehicles, satellite communication provides consistent connectivity and much wider coverage—especially in rural areas, mountainous regions, and oceans where deploying roadside units is difficult \cite{liu2021integration}. This significantly improves the scalability and accessibility of the transportation infrastructure. However, satellite communication also presents challenges, including difficulties in maintaining low latency and high bandwidth, as well as reduced reliability during severe weather conditions such as heavy rain \cite{ma2023network, alam2019estimation}.

% With the recent development and deployment of 5G networks, intelligent transportation systems can now achieve significantly improved performance in terms of communication speed, reliability, and scalability. Compared to satellite communication—which often suffers from high latency and weather-related disruptions—5G offers low-latency, high-bandwidth, and stable connections between vehicles, RSUs, and the cloud. These advancements enable real-time functions such as danger warnings, cooperative perception and cooperative decision-making. When combined with cloud computing, the system becomes more responsive, modular, and capable of scaling alongside the expansion of both indoor and outdoor mobility networks.

Building on these communication challenges, recent mass developments in cloud computing, edge devices, wireless connectivity, and especially in 5G networks \cite{yu2015intelligent} have enabled a new generation of ITS architecture, i.e., the 5G-based cloud autonomous mobile system\cite{yang2024intelligentmobilityintegratedmotion, ning2024enhancingindoormobilityconnected,ning2025sapcopesocialawareplanningusing}. Compared to the conventional communication system, the 5G communication technology allows massive Machine-Type Communications (mMTC) \cite{aladdin2023networkslices}, which supports massive sensor connectivity up to one million devices per kilometer square. The 5G technology also allows Ultra-Reliable Low Latency Communications (URLLC) abilities \cite{aladdin2023networkslices}, such that the latency can be decreased to one millisecond or even sub-ms with $>$99.999 reliability. Furthermore, the 5G technology also has several	cybersecurity advantages, such as real-time threat detection and response, enhanced data encryption and integrity, etc.
