\subsection{System Architecture}

The proposed CAM system is composed of two primary layers: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item A \textbf{distributed infrastructure sensor node network}, and 
    \item A \textbf{centralized cloud computing unit}.
\end{itemize}

An overview of the system architecture is illustrated in Fig.~\ref{Fig: framework}. The infrastructure sensor nodes—deployed in both outdoor urban environments and indoor facilities—collect local perception data using LiDAR and camera sensors. Each node processes this data locally and transmits compact perception outputs via 5G communication to a central cloud server. 

The cloud server fuses information from multiple nodes to create a unified global perception, supports real-time trajectory planning, and disseminates control and safety messages back to connected entities such as autonomous vehicles and robotic platforms over 5G network.

% For outdoor scenarios, nodes operate in the 28~GHz mmWave band to support high data throughput, while indoor nodes use the 3.5~GHz band for better wall penetration and indoor coverage. This unified architecture supports a diverse range of autonomous mobility applications under a common cloud infrastructure.

\subsection{Infrastructure Sensor Nodes}

Each infrastructure sensor node is designed as a modular and self-contained unit comprising three main components:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Sensing Module}: A combination of LiDAR and cameras that capture 3D spatial and visual information of the environment.
    \item \textbf{Compute Module}: An onboard processor responsible for sensor synchronization, multi-modal data fusion, object detection, and metadata generation.
    \item \textbf{Communication Module}: A 5G modem that transmits processed perception data to the cloud with minimal latency.
\end{itemize}

As shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig: Info_Flow}, each node performs local sensor fusion—aligning LiDAR point clouds and camera images through time synchronization and extrinsic calibration. Then the global perception information is send to the computing cloud center. 
%This enables the extraction of compact features such as object type, position, velocity, and tracking ID, which are efficiently transmitted to the cloud.
This distributed edge processing approach reduces the burden on cloud bandwidth, lowers system latency, and enhances overall scalability and robustness.

