\subsection{Cloud Computing and Information Fusion}

The cloud server receives perception data streams from all connected nodes and performs centralized information fusion. A global fusion module merges multi-source sensor (LiDARs and cameras) inputs into a coherent spatial map, enabling accurate tracking and forecasting of object trajectories across a broad area. As presented in Fig.~\ref{Fig: Info_Flow}, for outdoor applications, perception and hazard warning information is sent to human-driven vehicles (NV), and supporting planning information is sent to autonomous vehicles (AV). For indoor applications, planning and asset management information is sent to mobile robots for advanced autonomous mobility.


The cloud unit performs several key functions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Global-level data fusion and consistency maintenance
    \item Multi-agent tracking and prediction
    \item Detection of critical traffic events or anomalies
    \item Generation of planning commands for connected vehicles and robots
\end{itemize}

The processed outputs are relayed back to end-users—vehicles, hospital beds, or facility managers—via 5G networks, ensuring timely delivery of insights and instructions.

\subsection{Advantages of the CAM System}

The CAM framework offers several unique advantages over conventional perception architectures that rely solely on onboard sensors or isolated roadside units:

\begin{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Low-Latency Communication:} Leveraging 5G connectivity, the system achieves fast, reliable data transmission between infrastructure and vehicles. This enables real-time response with minimal delay across large areas.
    \item \textbf{Extended Perception Coverage:} High-mounted infrastructure sensors provide unobstructed fields of view, minimizing occlusion effects and enhancing object detection accuracy—particularly at intersections, corners, or indoor corridors.
    \item \textbf{Modularity and Scalability:} The plug-and-play nature of sensor nodes and distributed computing allows for easy system expansion. Additional nodes can be integrated without overloading the cloud, and regional processing ensures scalable operation across large deployments.
    %\item \textbf{Real-Time Safety and Navigation Support:} The fusion of distributed sensing and centralized intelligence enables the system to issue hazard warnings and generate optimized trajectories in real-time. This capability improves situational awareness and reduces the risk of accidents in both outdoor and indoor environments. 
    \item \textbf{Real-Time Safety and Navigation Support:} The system supports both CAVs and human-driven vehicles by transmitting enhanced decision-making data to CAVs and hazard warnings to human drivers. Infrastructure-based sensing reduces the need for onboard sensors, lowering costs and enabling broader deployment.
\end{itemize}

In summary, the CAM system bridges local sensing, high-speed networking, and cloud-level intelligence to support cooperative, safe, and efficient mobility in a wide range of transportation scenarios.


