\begin{abstract}
% In the field of natural language processing, large language models (LLMs) like GPT and LaMDA have significantly advanced the capabilities of machine understanding and generation. However, these models often produce erroneous outputs when dealing with scenarios requiring rare or specialized knowledge and struggle to reflect the most recent information. Recent research has attempted to address these issues by integrating information retrieval systems into LLMs. Despite these efforts, existing methods based on single retrieval and prompts still face significant limitations in handling multi-hop open-domain question answering (ODQA) tasks. In this paper, we propose Optimizing \textbf{Q}uestion Semantic Space for \textbf{D}ynamic \textbf{Re}trieval-\textbf{A}ugmented \textbf{M}ulti-hop Question Answering (\textbf{\texttt{Q-DREAM}}), a framework designed to enhance model performance through three modules to optimized retrieval of fine-grained subquestions. Our experiments across multiple  ODQA datasets demonstrate that the Q-DREAM framework not only significantly improves performance in both in-domain and out-of-domain settings but also achieves substantial gains on smaller models (such as Llama2-7b).
% \end{abstract}

% Large language models (LLMs) have revolutionized natural language processing but struggle with generating accurate responses for multi-hop open-domain questions requiring specialized or up-to-date knowledge. Recent efforts integrate retrieval systems with LLMs to mitigate hallucinations and knowledge obsolescence, yet existing methods face limitations in handling interdependent subquestions and semantic mismatches between queries and passages. To address these challenges, we propose Q-DREAM, a framework that optimizes the semantic space of questions for dynamic retrieval-augmented multi-hop question answering. Q-DREAM introduces three modules: the Question Decomposition Module breaks complex questions into fine-grained subquestions, the Subquestion Dependency Optimizer Module resolves inter-subquestion dependencies using retrieved contexts, and the Dynamic Passage Retrieval Module aligns subquestions with semantically clustered passages via adaptive LoRA-enhanced embeddings. By bridging semantic gaps and enabling context-aware retrieval, Q-DREAM reduces irrelevant retrievals and enhances reasoning coherence. Experiments on multi-hop QA datasets (HotpotQA, 2WikiMQA, IIRC) demonstrate that Q-DREAM achieves state-of-the-art performance, improving exact match scores by 6.7\% over prior methods on ChatGPT and boosting smaller models like Llama2-7b by 30.7\% EM. Notably, the framework exhibits strong cross-domain generalizability and reduces retrieval latency by 6× compared to interactive baselines, underscoring its efficiency and robustness.

% In the field of open-domain multi-hop question answering (ODQA), large language models (LLMs) have made significant progress in understanding and generating responses. However, existing approaches that integrate retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) with LLMs often struggle with multi-hop reasoning due to retrieval mismatches and the inability to handle interdependent subquestions. To address these challenges, we propose Optimizing Question Semantic Space for Dynamic Retrieval-Augmented Multi-hop Question Answering (Q-DREAM), a novel framework designed to enhance retrieval effectiveness through structured question decomposition and dynamic passage retrieval. Q-DREAM consists of three key modules: the Question Decomposition Module (QDM), which breaks down complex questions into fine-grained subquestions; the Subquestion Dependency Optimizer Module (SDOM), which refines interdependent subquestions for improved retrieval accuracy; and the Dynamic Passage Retrieval Module (DPRM), which aligns subquestions with relevant passages using optimized semantic embeddings. Experimental results across multiple ODQA datasets demonstrate that Q-DREAM significantly outperforms existing retrieval-augmented methods, achieving state-of-the-art performance in both in-domain and out-of-domain settings. Notably, Q-DREAM also improves retrieval efficiency while maintaining high accuracy, making it effective even for smaller-scale models like Llama2-7B.

Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) is usually integrated into large language models (LLMs) to mitigate hallucinations and knowledge obsolescence. Whereas, conventional one-step retrieve-and-read methods are insufficient for multi-hop question answering, facing challenges of retrieval semantic mismatching and the high cost in handling interdependent subquestions. In this paper, we propose Optimizing Question Semantic Space for Dynamic Retrieval-Augmented Multi-hop Question Answering (\texttt{Q-DREAM}). \texttt{Q-DREAM} consists of three key modules: (1) the Question Decomposition Module (\textit{QDM}), which decomposes multi-hop questions into fine-grained subquestions; (2) the Subquestion Dependency Optimizer Module (\textit{SDOM}), which models the interdependent relations of subquestions for better understanding; and (3) the Dynamic Passage Retrieval Module (\textit{DPRM}), which aligns subquestions with relevant passages by optimizing the semantic embeddings.
Experimental results across various benchmarks demonstrate that \texttt{Q-DREAM} significantly outperforms existing RAG methods, achieving state-of-the-art performance in both in-domain and out-of-domain settings. Notably, \texttt{Q-DREAM} also improves retrieval efficiency while maintaining high accuracy compared with recent baselines.
\end{abstract} 
