\subsubsection{Spectral Adaptation for LPI}

Figure~\ref{fig:fig3_spectrum_comparison} demonstrates the successful adaptation of spectral characteristics as the LPI weight $\lambda$ increases. For $\lambda = 0$ (PSL optimization only), the spectrum exhibits high concentration with distinct peaks (spectral variance $2.02 \times 10^{-5}$), making it easily detectable by electronic warfare systems. The balanced case at $\lambda = 0.5$ shows intermediate uniformity with variance $3.03 \times 10^{-6}$. As $\lambda$ increases to 2.0, the spectrum becomes progressively more uniform, with spectral variance decreasing to $8.02 \times 10^{-7}$---a 25-fold improvement over the PSL-only case. The final spectrum approaches noise-like characteristics, achieving the LPI objective while maintaining excellent radar performance.

