\subsection{AI Safety: Ensuring the Building's Inherent Soundness and Benign Operation}
AI Safety parallels the responsibilities of architects, structural engineers, and planners ensuring a building is \textit{inherently safe by design and in normal operation}. This involves preventing accidental harm, much as AI Safety aims to prevent unintended negative outcomes from AI systems (detailed in \S\ref{sec:research_focuses}).
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Alignment with Human Values:} Like ensuring a hospital's design prioritizes patient care over mere aesthetics, AI alignment ensures an AI's objectives match human intent.
    \item \textbf{Robustness (Non-Adversarial):} As a building withstands unexpected storms, a robust AI performs reliably under unforeseen, non-malicious conditions.
    \item \textbf{Transparency and Interpretability:} Clear blueprints and labeled utilities in a building allow for diagnosis and safe navigation, akin to XAI making AI decisions comprehensible.
    \item \textbf{Mitigating Ethical Risks:} Just as building design should ensure accessibility and avoid negative societal impacts, AI safety addresses fairness and bias.
    \item \textbf{Fail-Safes and Graceful Degradation:} Emergency systems in buildings (sprinklers, emergency exits) are like AI fail-safes that prevent catastrophic outcomes during internal malfunctions.
    \item \textbf{Safety Guardrails (Inherent Protections):} Building codes, non-toxic materials, and regular inspections are like AI safety guardrails constraining AI behavior to prevent harm.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{AI Security: Protecting the Building from Malicious Actors and Intentional Threats}
AI Security is analogous to the work of a security director fortifying the building against \textit{intentional, malicious threats} like break-ins or sabotage, mirroring AI Security's concerns (detailed in \S\ref{sec:research_focuses}).
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Adversarial Defense:} Protecting building systems (e.g., smart cameras, CCTV) from being tricked by intruders is like defending AI models against deceptive inputs.
    \item \textbf{Data Integrity (Preventing Sabotage):} Ensuring no one uses flawed materials or alters blueprints to weaken a building parallels protecting AI training data from poisoning.
    \item \textbf{Model Protection (Safeguarding Blueprints):} Protecting a building's unique designs from theft is like safeguarding proprietary AI models.
    \item \textbf{Access Control:} Locks and security badges restricting building access are like measures ensuring only authorized users interact with an AI system.
    \item \textbf{Resilience to Attacks:} A building maintaining essential functions during an attack (e.g., protestors blocking entrances) is like an AI service resisting a DDoS attack.
    \item \textbf{Security Guardrails (Active Defenses):} Intrusion detection systems, surveillance, and reinforced doors are active building defenses, similar to AI security guardrails like input sanitization.
\end{itemize}
Ultimately, a building can be structurally sound (safe) but still be burgled (insecure), or have excellent locks (secure) but a faulty electrical system (unsafe). %hs{just to confirm, you meant to say `electrical system', right?}. 
\textbf{An ideal AI system, like an ideal building, must be both robustly safe by design and strongly secured against malicious intent}. 

