		Some elementary properties of the core of product are as follows: 
		\begin{proposition}
			If $G,H$ are (di)graphs, 

			\begin{itemize}
				\item $\core{G \times G} = \core G$
				\item $G \ra H \Ra \core{G \times H} = \core G $
				\item $ |G \times H| = |G| \times |H|$
				\item $G \rra \core G$
			\end{itemize}
		\end{proposition}

		A less trivial but still well-known fact is the following.

		\begin{proposition}
			Given integers $n_1,n_2, \dots, n_k$ the core of the product of the directed cycles $C_{n_1},C_{n_2}, \dots, C_{n_k},$ is the directed cycle $C_r$ where $r=lcm(n_1,n_2, \dots, n_k)$ is the least common multiple of $n_1,n_2, \dots, \text{ and } n_k$.
		\end{proposition}

		Combined with the prime number theorem then this is enough to show the existence of oriented graphs of order at most $n$ whose core of product has size exponential in terms of $n$. 



