Motivated by this gap, we hypothesize that combining reinforcement learning with a FunSearch-like approach can exploit the strengths of both paradigms, enabling more effective algorithm discovery. 
% Our goal is to enable LLMs to learn from the solutions discovered during evolutionary search. 
Similarly to alignment methods \citep{ouyang2022training, stiennon2020learning}, we propose to train the LLM \emph{in-weight} using the evaluation scores of the generated programs as the reward signal. By adding in-weight training, we aim to enable the model to better utilize insights gained from exploration to improve its understanding of the search space and therefore enable better-targeted search at subsequent iterations.



Our contributions are summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item To the best of our knowledge, we are the first to demonstrate the potential of tightly integrating LLM-based evolutionary search with RL in the loop. Our method \methodname{} uses evolutionary search as an exploration strategy and RL to optimize and improve the policy. For updating the policy, we employ the DPO algorithm \citep{rafailov2024direct}; however, in contrast to its standard offline formulation, we leverage it in an off-policy setting with non-fixed inputs.
    %RL training with LLM-based evolutionary search for algorithm discovery.    
    % We demonstrate for the first time (to the best of our knowledge) the potential of integrating RL training with LLM-based evolutionary search for algorithm discovery. %(Section \ref{})
    \item 
    We improve the efficiency of the search mechanism, enabling the discovery of superior algorithms with reduced optimality gaps, as demonstrated in the bin packing, traveling salesman and flatpack problems. Our experiments spanning three instruction-tuned LLMs show that \methodname{} consistently outperforms the baseline FunSearch method.
    % By refining the LLM’s policy with RL, our approach accelerates the discovery of high-quality algorithms. In our experiments spanning three instruction-tuned LLMs and two combinatorial optimization problems, our method \methodname{} consistently outperforms standard evolutionary search.
    \item We show the advantage of using a modified version of the standard alignment objective in terms of preserving output diversity, which is critical to the success of evolutionary search strategies.
