% \paragraph{Evolutionary search with LLMs}
%~\citet{lehman2023evolution} introduce ELM as an evolutionary method for generating Python programs determining the configuration of working ambulating robots, and leverage RL \emph{only} for conditional construction of such programs in novel domains and not for improving the search mechanism. In contrast, we tightly integrate RL and evolutionary exploration for improving the generator policy and finding superior programs.
%~\citet{fei2024eoh} jointly evolve heuristic programs and their corresponding \emph{thoughts} with LLM without any training.~\citet{ye2024reevo} further extend this idea by using a self-reflecting LLM that extracts insights and cues corresponding to higher-performing programs through comparison and uses such insights for mutating heuristics.~\citet{liu2023algorithm} propose an evolutionary framework for generating optimization algorithms, relying on prompt engineering for mutation and crossover without any training or use of reward scores.~\citet{liu2023large} introduce LMEA, which also heavily relies on carefully curated prompts and directly evolves solutions described in natural language, not the algorithm, which does not scale with problem size.

\paragraph{Evolutionary search with LLMs}
~\citet{lehman2023evolution} introduce ELM for evolving Python programs that configure walking robots, using RL \emph{only} to condition generation in new domains -- not to improve the search itself. In contrast, we integrate RL with evolutionary search to refine the generator policy.~\citet{fei2024eoh} evolve heuristics and their \emph{thoughts} with a LLM without training.~\citet{ye2024reevo} extend this with a self-reflecting LLM and specialized evolutionary steps.~\citet{liu2023algorithm} evolve optimization algorithms via prompt-based mutation and crossover without reward feedback.~\citet{liu2023large} propose LMEA, which relies on carefully curated prompts and evolves natural language solutions not algorithms which limiting scalability.

\paragraph{Prompt optimization}
A closely related line of research explores how to vary and optimize the prompts to better elicit desirable outputs from LLMs.~\citet{yang2024large} leverage the LLM as a prompt optimizer that directly outputs solutions as a black-box method without iterating over algorithms and without updating the generator policy.~\citet{guo2023connecting} introduce EvoPrompt, which integrates evolutionary search with LLMs for prompt optimization without a learning component. Similarly,~\citet{fernando2023promptbreeder} developed an evolutionary method that self-referentially evolves and improves the prompts and mutation operators jointly. 
% Such approaches are complementary to our work, as they a) focus on refining prompts to elicit desirable behavior from LLMs, and b) focus on general language understanding and generation capabilities, whereas we emphasize on integrating RL with evolutionary search with untuned prompts for algorithm discovery. Nevertheless, incorporating such prompt optimization techniques into \methodname\ could potentially further improve its effectiveness, making this an interesting direction for future investigation.

\paragraph{Neural combinatorial optimization (NCO)} NCO is an important orthogonal class of methods that learn to construct solutions for combinatorial optimization problems using embeddings of problem instances as input. Such models can be trained with supervised learning~\citep{vinyals2015pointer,joshi2019efficient,fu2021generalize,joshi2022rethinking,kool2022deep,hottung2021learning} or RL~\citep{bello2016neural,kool2018attention,hottung2020neural,hottung2021efficient,chen2019learning} (See~\citet{luo2023neural} for more references). Our work on the other hand does not deal with problem instances directly; rather, it searches for an algorithm or heuristic that can later be utilized with problems of any size.

\paragraph{Self-improvement and self-training} 
Iterative self-improvement training techniques work by training models using their own generated outputs \citep{zelikman2022star, gulcehre2023reinforced, singh2023beyond}. Candidate solutions are generated and then filtered based on correctness or alignment with predefined criteria. The selected outputs are used to fine-tune the model, and this cycle is repeated, gradually improving its ability to produce desirable solutions. Our method adds to the repertoire of self-improving techniques -- by training the LLM on self-discovered solutions, we improve the evolutionary search capabilities of the model.

\section{Conclusion} 
We found that existing evolutionary search approaches can converge to suboptimal solutions with a limited computational budget. To address this, we introduced \methodname{}, a novel approach that integrates evolutionary search with RL fine-tuning to improve LLM-driven algorithm discovery. By iteratively refining the LLM through RL finetuning, our method outperforms purely search-based baseline on well-known and challenging combinatorial optimization tasks with a fixed sampling budget.

Although our results highlight the promise of \methodname{}, several questions remain for further investigation. Our experiments were conducted with LLMs ranging from 1B to 3.8B parameters and with a sampling budget of up to 22.4k outputs. Further scaling of both the model size and sampling budget is needed to fully understand the method's potential. Furthermore, while \methodname{} discovers better solutions within a fixed sampling budget, it incurs additional compute costs due to the RL training phase. Investigating the trade-offs between training and inference costs, especially at larger scales, is an important direction for future research.

% Moreover, we conjecture that task complexity might further influence the performance gap between \methodname{} and FunSearch, especially in scenarios were pure evolutionary search cannot utilize the signal from exploration of the search space efficiently enough. As a future direction, we would like to extend our evaluation to other tasks -- in particular those with exceptionally challenging optimization spaces and intricate symmetries -- to further examine the versatility of our method. In that regard, we propose to investigate the task of designing error-correcting codes (Boolean sphere packing) \citep{macwilliams1977theory}; this corresponds to designing a collection of $M$ binary vectors in ambient dimension $n$ with the largest Hamming distance. 
% Furthermore, as our method allows for the integration of a variety of RL algorithms, exploring alternatives could further improve its performance. 

In a nutshell, \methodname{} demonstrates the potential of combining the synergistic strengths of evolutionary search and reinforcement learning, paving the way for future advances in LLM-based algorithm discovery. 


